messages:
marta: quick q — for the /thanks page after webinar signups: we need to capture UTMs into HubSpot, avoid duplicate indexing across querystring variants, and surface a clean canonical for search. do we canonicalize to the webinar hub (/webinars/ai-inference) or set noindex on the thanks page?

jason: i wouldn't index the raw /thanks?utm= pages. two options: 1) canonicalize to the main webinar landing (/webinars/ai-inference) and keep the thanks page noindexed, or 2) canonical to the thanks page without query params (strip utm) if that page has unique content. lean 1 for fewer indexing signals. :thumbsup:

li: agree with canonical -> hub for SEO simplicity. but we still want UTMs to persist in analytics/CRM. recommend capturing UTMs in GTM/dataLayer then firing a HubSpot event. also add a small JSON-LD Event snippet on the hub page (not on the noindexed thank-you) so search can surface event info.

marco: implementation notes from eng side: we can return a canonical header and set <link rel=\"canonical\"> in the HTML that strips query params. example: `link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://redwood.com/webinars/ai-inference\"` for /thanks pages. for CRMs, push dataLayer like: `window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || []; dataLayer.push({event: 'hs_form_submit', utm_source: getParam('utm_source'), utm_medium: getParam('utm_medium'), utm_campaign: getParam('utm_campaign')});` then GTM maps to HubSpot.

emily-marketing: perfect — routing rules: utm_campaign -> lifecycle (seed|webinar|benchmark), utm_source -> channel (newsletter|paid|partner), utm_content -> creative_id. i'll draft the mapping sheet and post it here. can we also capture the original landing path so we can dedupe leads by landing_url + email?

jason: yes capture `document.referrer` + `location.pathname` into dataLayer too. one more SEO guard: return `X-Robots-Tag: noindex,follow` header for urls with query strings that serve thanks content (helps crawlers).

seo-bot: automated check: /thanks pages with query params should not be indexable by default. running scan would flag any that expose canonical inconsistencies.

marta: thanks — can someone paste a minimal JSON-LD sample for the webinar event and a tiny GTM snippet we can drop into the implementation ticket? i'll add to the post-launch checklist once we confirm.

marco: JSON-LD (put this on the webinar hub page, keep it brief):

```
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Event",
  "name": "AI Inference: Production Best Practices",
  "startDate": "2026-05-12T16:00:00-07:00",
  "location": {"@type": "VirtualLocation", "url": "https://redwood.com/webinars/ai-inference"},
  "offers": {"@type": "Offer", "url": "https://redwood.com/register?utm_source=newsletter"}
}
```

marco: GTM/dataLayer quick snippet for the thanks page (example):

```
<script>
(function(){
  function q(name){
    var m = new RegExp('[?&]'+name+'=([^&]*)').exec(location.search);
    return m ? decodeURIComponent(m[1]) : '';
  }
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  dataLayer.push({
    event: 'redwood_webinar_thanks',
    utm_source: q('utm_source'),
    utm_medium: q('utm_medium'),
    utm_campaign: q('utm_campaign'),
    landing_path: sessionStorage.getItem('original_path') || document.referrer || location.pathname
  });
})();
</script>
```

li: small note — for GDPR regions, ensure we only push identifiable data after consent. keep the dataLayer fields minimal.

emily-marketing: i'll add the mapping doc and a consent check row. then i'll open a ticket for eng + analytics to implement canonical header + GTM push.

jason: i'll add `X-Robots-Tag` to the ticket and ask infra to make sure the canonical header is consistent for server-side navigations.

marta: awesome, thanks team. i'll mark this as blocked until the mapping doc and a small QA checklist are in the ticket (can we target next sprint?). :raised_hands:
